  a_1(2500)/a_1(500): median 2.96, 16-84% [2.08, 3.35], scatter 0.109 dex
  [FAIL] 6a CAN FAIL, AND IT IS THE CLEANEST TEST OF A SECOND ACCELERATION ANYWHERE IN THE HUNT: the a_1 solved at Delta = 2500 must equal the a_1 solved at Delta = 500 in the same object.  No fit, no profile model, two measured mass points   (median ratio 2.96 (+0.471 dex) over 20 groups)
  f_star scan of the two-overdensity ratio: f*=0.005 -> 3.34, f*=0.010 -> 3.17, f*=0.020 -> 2.96, f*=0.035 -> 2.71, f*=0.050 -> 2.52, f*=0.080 -> 2.26
  d log[a_1(2500)/a_1(500)] / d log f_star = -0.138; to drive the measured +0.471 dex to zero would need f_star wrong by 3.41 dex = a factor 2598, which would put the stars above the total mass
  [PASS] 6a2 THE KILL'S ROBUSTNESS: the only assumed number in the two-overdensity test is the stellar fraction, and it cannot rescue a second acceleration   (f_star would have to be wrong by 3.41 dex)
  eta at Delta=2500: median 2.38, scatter 0.058 dex, d log eta/d log M500 = +0.075
  eta at Delta= 500: median 1.45, scatter 0.054 dex, d log eta/d log M500 = +0.152
  [FAIL] 6b CAN FAIL: at FIXED overdensity, is eta independent of cluster mass?  (At fixed Delta, g_bar runs as M^(1/3), so a mass-independent eta at fixed Delta is what an overdensity law predicts and an acceleration law forbids)   (d log eta(Delta=500)/d log M500 = +0.152 over 7.0x in mass; g_bar itself moves 1.9x over that range)

6c. WHAT SURVIVES: eta at FIXED overdensity, two independent samples, 1.7 decades of mass
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    sample                       N   mass range [M_sun]        eta(Delta=500)        scatter
    Lovisari+2015 groups (X-ray)  20   2.1e+13 - 1.4e+14   1.454                 0.054 dex
    X-COP clusters (measured M*)   7   4.6e+14 - 8.9e+14   1.463                 0.053 dex
    JOINED                        27   2.1e+13 - 8.9e+14   1.459                 0.053 dex   (d log eta/d log M500 = +0.022, residual scatter 0.052 dex)
  [PASS] 6c1 CAN FAIL: is eta at Delta = 500 the SAME number in two independent samples measured by different groups, over 1.7 decades of mass?   (groups 1.454 vs clusters 1.463 (-0.0028 dex apart); joined scatter 0.053 dex)
  [PASS] 6c2 CAN FAIL: is the joined mass trend consistent with zero over 1.7 decades?   (d log eta(Delta=500)/d log M500 = +0.022 over 43x in mass)
  per-object eta(2500)/eta(500) = 1.663, scatter 0.053 dex, d log/d log M500 = -0.078
  [PASS] 6c3 the residual's DEPENDENCE ON OVERDENSITY is itself universal: the same object's eta rises by the same factor between Delta = 500 and Delta = 2500 in every group   (eta(2500)/eta(500) = 1.663 +- 0.053 dex over 20 groups)

    THE GROUPS' OWN UPSILON LEVER: Lovisari gives no stellar masses, so f_star had to be assumed.  Scanned:
      f_star = 0.005 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.581 +- 0.059 dex   eta(2500) = 2.841   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = +0.033 dex
      f_star = 0.010 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.535 +- 0.057 dex   eta(2500) = 2.662   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = +0.021 dex
      f_star = 0.020 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.454 +- 0.054 dex   eta(2500) = 2.382   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = -0.003 dex
      f_star = 0.035 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.352 +- 0.050 dex   eta(2500) = 2.069   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = -0.034 dex
      f_star = 0.050 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.268 +- 0.048 dex   eta(2500) = 1.855   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = -0.062 dex
      f_star = 0.080 of M500:  eta(500) = 1.135 +- 0.045 dex   eta(2500) = 1.567   offset from the X-COP clusters (measured stars) = -0.111 dex
      (X-COP's own measured stellar masses correspond to M*/M500 = 0.009)

    THE RESTATEMENT TEST FOR THIS ONE, DONE EXPLICITLY AND AGAINST INTEREST:
    In the deep-MOND limit nu = sqrt(a_0/g_bar), so
        eta = (M_dyn/M_bar)/nu = (1/f_b) sqrt(g_bar/a_0) = sqrt( g_N /(a_0 f_b) )   with  f_b = M_bar/M_dyn,
    hence  eta^2 f_b a_0 / g_N(R500) = 1 IDENTICALLY.  So 'eta(Delta=500) is universal' is algebraically the SAME
    statement as 'f_gas a_0/g_N(R500) is constant', which is this programme's own item 19 (flat to -0.010 +- 0.005
    in eRASS1) -- and that in turn is the MEASURED gas-fraction-mass relation f_b ~ M^(1/3) wearing a_0's clothes.
  numerical check of the identity eta^2 f_b a_0/g_N = 1 in the deep limit: median 0.862 (it is not exactly 1 because the kernel is only ~24% from its asymptote at these accelerations, y = 0.022)
  [PASS] 6c4 THE RESTATEMENT TEST CLOSES for this half of the item: eta at fixed overdensity is an algebraic rewrite of the measured gas-fraction-mass relation, so it must be LABELLED a restatement, not a new law   (eta^2 f_b a_0/g_N = 0.862 over the 20 groups; the deep-MOND identity would make it exactly 1)

7.  MUTATION CONTROLS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] M1 a_0 x 4 must move the crossing overdensity -- the estimator is not a_0-blind   (Delta_1(4 a_0) = 1295.0 vs 47.1 (+1.439 dex))
  with nu = 1 (pure Newton) eta = M_dyn/M_bar >= 5 everywhere in every cluster (min over the sample 2.78), so no crossing exists at all
  [PASS] M2 with no kernel (nu = 1) there is no crossing anywhere in any cluster, so r_1 is a property of the kernel and not of the mass profiles   (min M_dyn/M_bar over the whole sample = 2.78)
  [PASS] M3 pairing control: giving each cluster ANOTHER cluster's baryon profile must inflate the scatter in Delta_1 if the tightness is carried by the pairing   (shuffled scatter 2.353 dex vs real 1.773 dex)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k02
======================================================================================================================
  Delta_1 (canonical) = 47.1 +- 1.773 dex over 7 X-COP clusters
  g_bar(r_1)/a_0      = 0.022 +- 1.251 dex
  r_1/R500            = 2.45 +- 0.587 dex
  Delta_1 rho_c(z) / rho_DE = 73.44
  the two-overdensity group test: a_1(2500)/a_1(500) = 2.96 +- 0.109 dex, 20/20 groups the same sign
  WHAT DIES: r_1 (100% extrapolated, unstable, and LambdaCDM produces a tighter Delta_1 than the data).
  WHAT DIES: any single second ACCELERATION for clusters -- killed by an over-determined two-point test.
  WHAT SURVIVES, LABELLED: eta(Delta) is universal -- eta(500) = 1.459 +- 0.053 dex over 1.7 decades of mass in two samples, eta(2500) = 2.382 +- 0.058 dex -- but it is
  an algebraic rewrite of the measured gas-fraction-mass relation and must be quoted as a restatement.

